WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.300
Kyle Moretti, LA Film School Student: When
writing a story do you think it’s important

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:08.680
to write the story around a transmedia structure
or to extend it to transmedia after you have

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.030
already developed the story?

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:14.860
Houston Howard, Transmedia Author/Speaker:
So when you are developing stories I think

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:25.310
you have to understand that you have to execute
this single story by itself no matter what

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.550
other transmedia idea that you have.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:39.370
I think the tendency can be I get so focused
on all the stories that I don’t execute

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:49.690
any individual story the right way and that’s
a mistake because you have to understand if

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.510
your movie isn’t good, if your script isn’t
good nothing works.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.120
If your TV pilot good nothing works.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:07.950
Now what I would say is I’m a fan of developing
the transmedia plan first and understanding

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:13.079
how all the stories interplay and understanding
your story world and understand where the

00:01:13.079 --> 00:01:18.909
opportunities are and then once you have that
sort of structure and that architecture at

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:27.060
play and you understand what part of the puzzle
this individual story is, then what you do

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.810
is set that aside, you set all that aside
and then you’ve just got to dive in and

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:38.889
make sure you write an awesome script and
an awesome pilot and execute that thing.

00:01:38.889 --> 00:01:47.869
But when you’re writing it you will naturally
be building in new opportunities because you’ll

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:50.630
understand how this interplays with the other
stories, you’ll understand more about your

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:55.100
story world and naturally you’re going to
start lacing things in that if you hadn’t

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.869
done that work first you wouldn’t have which
means then if you do all of the transmedia

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:05.429
opportunities after the fact one or two things
are going to happen is you’re probably going

00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:09.750
to say “Oh I need to go rewrite my script
to be able to accommodate for all of these

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:10.750
things.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.390
But then it may not feel natural or organic.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.930
Or you are just going to say “I’m going
to leave it as it is,” and then it feels

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:20.790
disconnected and you are not going to get
the experience that you want.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.049
It’s a balance.

00:02:22.049 --> 00:02:31.069
If you don’t develop it as you’re writing,
I think you develop the broad plan from an

00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:34.340
IP perspective this is my roadmap.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:40.360
If I’m going to drive from LA to New York
and I’m going to look at the map and I see

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.209
my route that I’m going to take (this is
the broad route that I’m going to take)

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:49.049
and I map that and I understand where I am
going, I understand how to get there but eventually

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:53.739
I need to get in the car and I’m not going
to be starting at the map the entire time

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:57.110
I’m in the car and I’ll make minor adjustments
as I’m in the car.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.980
I’ll get off at certain rest stops.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:04.819
I’ll maybe take a different road here but
I still already understand the direction that

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:05.819
I’m going.

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:08.980
The problem is I think most people jump in
the car and they don’t even have a destination.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.740
They just say “I’m just going to drive.”

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.530
That just ends up being more work on the back
end.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.280
Personally I hate to rewrite, I hate rewriting.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.159
I think all writers hate rewriting and so
I want to minimize my rewriting process as

00:03:21.159 --> 00:03:31.219
much as possible which means then I think
you just build an IP design, set that aside

00:03:31.219 --> 00:03:32.219
and then go.

00:03:32.219 --> 00:03:36.829
And I think that the balance of that is sometimes
tricky and you kind of learn your way but

00:03:36.829 --> 00:03:37.430
I think that’s the best way forward.

